    d("not_provided", "What did the second chapter say about taxes?", "I haven't been given any chapter in this conversation, so I can't say what it said. If you paste the text of chapter two, I'll summarise the tax part.", "It's about sales tax mostly. Quote: 'Sales tax is collected at the point of sale and remitted to the state quarterly.'", "From that sentence: sales tax is collected when the sale happens and then passed on to the state every quarter. That's all I can say about it, as the rest of the chapter is unseen by me.", "So how often is it paid?", "Quarterly, according to the line you pasted: four times a year.", "And is there a penalty for being late?", "The quote doesn't say. Penalties for late filing vary by place, so check the chapter or the tax authority's guidance rather than relying on me.")
    d("not_provided", "Can you compare my two drafts?", "I only see this message, not any drafts. Please paste both, labelled Draft 1 and Draft 2.", "Draft 1: 'We are happy to announce our new office.' Draft 2: 'Big news: we have moved into a new office!'", "Draft 1 is formal and neutral, suited to a press release or a client email. Draft 2 is lively and informal, suited to social media or a team post. Both are clear; Draft 2 adds energy but uses an exclamation mark and 'big news', which some audiences find casual.", "Which suits LinkedIn?", "LinkedIn is professional but personable, so a middle path works: \"We're excited to announce that we've moved into our new office.\" It keeps the formality of Draft 1 and the warmth of Draft 2.", "Combine them, then.", "\"We're excited to announce our move into a new office. Come and visit us!\" It keeps the announcement from Draft 1 and the friendly tone from Draft 2.")
